text:
RDS
RDS can preserve:

factor structure;
variable classes;
labels;
attributes;
R-specific objects.

CSV
CSV supports:

portability;
review outside R;
use in other analytical tools;
simple archival inspection.

The pipeline may treat RDS as the primary reusable format and generate CSV copies only when requested.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lesson, you should be able to:

distinguish an R pipeline error from a Quarto rendering error;
identify the first meaningful error message;
confirm that commands are being run from the project root;
diagnose package, path, download, and import failures;
investigate missing or duplicated participant keys;
diagnose row-binding and joining problems;
troubleshoot missing analytical variables;
troubleshoot figure-generation and figure-embedding problems;
inspect failed validation checks;
create a reproducible troubleshooting report;
know when to stop rather than forcing the pipeline to continue.

Why troubleshooting should be systematic
A long analytical pipeline can fail for many reasons.
The failure may involve:

the working directory;
a missing package;
a malformed source catalog;
an unavailable URL;
an incomplete download;
an invalid XPT file;
a missing variable;
duplicated keys;
incompatible variable classes;
an incorrect join;
a missing output directory;
a failed figure export;
a missing embedded image;
malformed Quarto YAML;
an invalid Markdown structure.

Trying random fixes can make the problem harder to diagnose.
A better approach is:
Identify the first meaningful failure
→ isolate the affected section
→ inspect the relevant object or file
→ correct the underlying cause
→ restart from a clean session
→ rerun the pipeline

The most important troubleshooting rule






ImportantFix the first error first



Later errors are often consequences of the first failure.
For example:

a source file fails to download;
the import object is not created;
the combined dataset is incomplete;
the merge fails;
the figures are not produced;
Quarto cannot find the PNG files.

The original problem is the failed download, not the missing figure.

R analytical pipeline
Run with:
Rscript nhanes_pipeline.R
This system:

downloads data;
imports XPT files;
validates records;
creates processed datasets;
generates tables;
generates figures.

Quarto website renderer
Run with:
quarto render
This system:

reads .qmd files;
processes YAML;
converts lessons to HTML;
copies figures and supporting files;
builds the website.

Common causes

missing closing parenthesis;
missing closing brace;
missing quotation mark;
extra comma;
incomplete pipe;
malformed function call;
Markdown code fences copied into the R script;
prose copied outside an R comment.

Identify failed sources in R
failed_downloads &lt;- acquisition_manifest |&gt;
  dplyr::filter(
    status != "success"
  )

print(
  failed_downloads,
  n = Inf
)

Check the local file
ls -lh data/raw
A failed download may produce:

no file;
a zero-byte file;
a very small file;
an HTML error page saved with an .xpt extension.

Common causes

incomplete download;
HTML response saved as XPT;
zero-byte file;
incorrect catalog URL;
corrupted local file;
file permission problem.

Possible causes

the wrong source file was downloaded;
the source is not person-level;
the source schema differs from the catalog declaration;
an HTML file was imported incorrectly;
the column was renamed or removed earlier in the pipeline.

Identify the duplicated keys
duplicate_keys &lt;- data |&gt;
  dplyr::count(
    survey_cycle,
    SEQN,
    name = "occurrences"
  ) |&gt;
  dplyr::filter(
    occurrences &gt; 1
  )

print(
  duplicate_keys,
  n = Inf
)

Extract affected records
duplicate_records &lt;- data |&gt;
  dplyr::semi_join(
    duplicate_keys,
    by = c(
      "survey_cycle",
      "SEQN"
    )
  ) |&gt;
  dplyr::arrange(
    survey_cycle,
    SEQN
  )

print(
  duplicate_records,
  n = Inf
)

Do not fix with arbitrary deduplication
Avoid:
dplyr::distinct(
  survey_cycle,
  SEQN,
  .keep_all = TRUE
)
The correct response depends on whether the repeated records are:

exact duplicates;
repeat examinations;
complementary records;
conflicting values;
incorrectly combined sources.

Diagnose the variable class by source
purrr::imap_dfr(
  primary_cbc_sources,
  function(
      data,
      dataset_id) {

    tibble::tibble(
      dataset_id = dataset_id,
      variable_class = paste(
        class(
          data$VARIABLE_NAME
        ),
        collapse = "; "
      )
    )
  }
)
Replace:
VARIABLE_NAME
with the actual failing variable.

Inspect values
purrr::imap(
  primary_cbc_sources,
  function(
      data,
      dataset_id) {

    list(
      dataset_id = dataset_id,
      values = unique(
        data$VARIABLE_NAME
      )
    )
  }
)

Possible causes

one cycle stores the variable as character;
another stores it as numeric;
one source contains labelled numeric data;
a local transformation changed one source;
a metadata column was created inconsistently.

Calculate source rows
source_row_counts &lt;- purrr::imap_dfr(
  primary_cbc_sources,
  function(
      data,
      dataset_id) {

    tibble::tibble(
      dataset_id = dataset_id,
      rows = nrow(
        data
      )
    )
  }
)
Inspect:
print(
  source_row_counts,
  n = Inf
)
Calculate total:
sum(
  source_row_counts$rows
)
Compare with:
nrow(
  cbc_primary_1999_2018
)

Possible causes

a source was omitted;
a source was included twice;
filtering occurred before binding;
the wrong source list was used;
a supplementary source entered the primary list;
a source object was overwritten.

Check CBC key uniqueness
cbc_premerge_duplicate_keys &lt;-
  cbc_primary_1999_2018 |&gt;
  dplyr::count(
    survey_cycle,
    SEQN,
    name = "occurrences"
  ) |&gt;
  dplyr::filter(
    occurrences &gt; 1
  )

Check Demographic key uniqueness
demographic_premerge_duplicate_keys &lt;-
  demographics_1999_2018 |&gt;
  dplyr::count(
    survey_cycle,
    SEQN,
    name = "occurrences"
  ) |&gt;
  dplyr::filter(
    occurrences &gt; 1
  )
Inspect both:
print(
  cbc_premerge_duplicate_keys,
  n = Inf
)
print(
  demographic_premerge_duplicate_keys,
  n = Inf
)

Identify the overlaps
overlapping_nonkey_columns &lt;- intersect(
  setdiff(
    names(
      cbc_for_merge
    ),
    merge_key
  ),
  setdiff(
    names(
      demographics_for_merge
    ),
    merge_key
  )
)
Inspect:
print(
  overlapping_nonkey_columns
)

Fix
Determine whether each overlap represents:

the same variable from two components;
different variables with the same name;
duplicated provenance metadata;
an accidental local column.

Rename component-specific provenance columns before joining.
Do not accept automatic suffixes without understanding the conflict.

Possible causes

the variable does not exist in one or more source cycles;
the wrong CBC file was used;
the variable was renamed;
it was removed during select();
a join conflict changed its name;
a source schema changed.

Possible causes

the dataset was exported to CSV and reloaded;
a transformation removed attributes;
the variable was recreated;
a conversion stripped the label.

Remember
CSV does not preserve R variable-label attributes.
Use:

RDS for analytical metadata;
CSV for portability;
the variable dictionary for documentation.

Possible causes

a source code was omitted from the factor levels;
a label was misspelled;
whitespace is present;
the factor level order is incomplete;
the source code differs from the expected coding.

Diagnose
Check group sizes:
hemoglobin_by_cycle_and_sex |&gt;
  dplyr::select(
    survey_cycle,
    sex,
    records,
    nonmissing_hemoglobin
  )
Check nonfinite values:
hemoglobin_by_cycle_and_sex |&gt;
  dplyr::filter(
    !is.finite(
      mean_hemoglobin
    ) |
      !is.finite(
        standard_deviation
      ) |
      !is.finite(
        standard_error
      )
  )

Possible causes

a group contains no nonmissing values;
a group contains only one observation;
the wrong object was summarized;
missing values were not handled;
a calculation divided by zero.

Typical symptoms

one line is missing;
both lines have the same color;
points do not align with intervals;
the legend is duplicated;
the order is incorrect.

Common causes

missing opening or closing ---;
tabs instead of spaces;
unmatched quotation marks;
incorrect indentation;
a colon in unquoted text;
an outer Markdown code fence around the file content.

Possible causes

the source file is not producing HTML;
the YAML requests a different format;
the document is malformed;
the file is wrapped in an outer code fence;
the source filename differs from the sidebar path;
hidden characters are present in the filename.

Why the browser shows connection refused
The preview server stops when:

the terminal is closed;
the process is interrupted;
Quarto exits after an error;
the computer sleeps;
another command kills the process.

quarto render does not keep a preview server running.

Symptoms

changes do not appear;
old text remains;
an old figure is displayed;
deleted pages still appear.

Likely cause
A sitewide CSS rule affects:

headings;
paragraphs;
cards;
navigation;
layout;
page width.

Cause
The interactive R session may already contain:

loaded packages;
helper functions;
imported data;
intermediate objects;
variables created during testing.

Fix
Ensure the script itself:

loads every required package;
sources every helper file;
creates every directory;
reads every configuration file;
creates every required object;
does not rely on the Global Environment.

Possible causes

export failed partway;
the source object was empty;
a connection was interrupted;
a placeholder file was created;
the process stopped before writing finished.

Explanation
CSV does not preserve:

factors;
ordered factors;
labels;
dates without parsing instructions;
other R attributes.

Potential causes

repeated downloads;
repeated hashing of large outputs;
repeated CSV exports;
high-resolution figure exports;
running from a network-synchronized folder;
antivirus or cloud backup scanning;
low available memory.

Safe improvements

reuse validated local raw files;
avoid rerunning during lesson writing;
debug one section at a time;
retain RDS files for faster reload;
run the complete pipeline only after changes are finalized.

Do not remove scientific validation merely to reduce runtime.

Symptoms

caption text is cut off;
the annotation is partly outside the figure;
axis labels are truncated;
the legend is clipped.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lesson, you should be able to:

create a reusable publication-style ggplot2 theme;
use a pure white background without gridlines;
use color and shape together to distinguish groups;
apply a transparent, data-informed vertical scale;
create a principal mean-trend figure;
display descriptive interval bars;
add an explicit in-figure caveat;
create supporting distribution, record-count, and missingness figures;
validate the data used in every graph;
save figures as PNG, PDF, and SVG files;
display generated figures directly inside a Quarto lesson;
distinguish professional presentation from valid survey inference.

Figures created in this lesson
This lesson creates four complementary figures.

Figure 1: Mean hemoglobin trend
The principal figure displays:

unweighted mean hemoglobin;
ten NHANES survey cycles;
separate male and female trends;
conventional descriptive intervals;
a focused but transparent vertical scale;
a prominent unweighted-analysis caveat.



Figure 2: Hemoglobin distributions
The distribution figure displays:

participant-level values;
medians;
interquartile ranges;
distributional spread;
possible extreme observations.



Figure 3: Analytical record counts
The record-count figure shows how many complete records contribute to each cycle-sex mean.


Figure 4: Hemoglobin missingness
The missingness figure shows the percentage of records without hemoglobin values by cycle and sex.

Important statistical scope
Every figure in this lesson is based on:
unweighted descriptive analysis
The figures do not incorporate:

NHANES survey weights;
masked variance strata;
masked primary sampling units;
multi-cycle weight construction;
complex-survey variance estimation.







WarningProfessional appearance does not make a result nationally representative



A figure can be visually suitable for a manuscript draft while still requiring additional statistical work.
These figures describe the analytical records and must not be presented as national population estimates.

Confirm the required plotting objects
Define:
required_figure_objects &lt;- c(
  "hemoglobin_trend_figure_data",
  "hemoglobin_distribution_figure_data",
  "hemoglobin_record_count_figure_data",
  "hemoglobin_missingness_figure_data",
  "trend_y_axis_lower",
  "trend_y_axis_upper",
  "trend_y_axis_break"
)
Identify missing objects:
missing_figure_objects &lt;- required_figure_objects[
  !purrr::map_lgl(
    required_figure_objects,
    exists
  )
]
Stop if any are absent:
if (
  length(
    missing_figure_objects
  ) &gt; 0
) {
  stop(
    "Required plotting objects are missing: ",
    paste(
      missing_figure_objects,
      collapse = ", "
    )
  )
}

Why use a custom theme?
The custom theme provides:

a pure white figure background;
no horizontal or vertical gridlines;
black axis lines and ticks;
clear title hierarchy;
readable axis labels;
consistent legend placement;
adequate margins for publication and export.

It also ensures that all four figures share the same visual identity.

Validate the principal plotting data
Check required columns:
required_trend_columns &lt;- c(
  "survey_cycle",
  "survey_cycle_factor",
  "sex",
  "records",
  "mean_hemoglobin",
  "lower_descriptive_95",
  "upper_descriptive_95"
)
Identify missing columns:
missing_trend_columns &lt;- setdiff(
  required_trend_columns,
  names(
    hemoglobin_trend_figure_data
  )
)
Stop if any are missing:
if (
  length(
    missing_trend_columns
  ) &gt; 0
) {
  stop(
    "Trend figure data are missing required columns: ",
    paste(
      missing_trend_columns,
      collapse = ", "
    )
  )
}

Confirm one row per cycle and sex
Run:
duplicate_trend_figure_groups &lt;-
  hemoglobin_trend_figure_data |&gt;
  dplyr::count(
    survey_cycle,
    sex,
    name = "rows"
  ) |&gt;
  dplyr::filter(
    rows &gt; 1
  )
Stop if duplicated:
if (
  nrow(
    duplicate_trend_figure_groups
  ) &gt; 0
) {
  stop(
    "The trend figure contains duplicate cycle-sex groups."
  )
}

Confirm that all values fit the scale
Run:
trend_values_outside_scale &lt;-
  hemoglobin_trend_figure_data |&gt;
  dplyr::filter(
    lower_descriptive_95 &lt;
      trend_y_axis_lower |
      upper_descriptive_95 &gt;
        trend_y_axis_upper
  )
Expected:
nrow(
  trend_values_outside_scale
)
[1] 0
Stop if needed:
if (
  nrow(
    trend_values_outside_scale
  ) &gt; 0
) {
  stop(
    "The selected y-axis scale excludes one or more trend intervals."
  )
}

Why this figure is more publication-ready
The figure uses:

a white background;
no gridlines;
distinct color and shape encoding;
clearly labelled units;
readable chronological cycles;
restrained line and point sizes;
a data-informed vertical scale;
an explicit in-figure warning;
a detailed scientific caption.

Why examine distributions?
Two groups can have similar means but very different:

medians;
spreads;
skewness;
extreme values;
interquartile ranges.

The distribution figure helps prevent overreliance on the mean trend.

Validate distribution data
Run:
distribution_figure_validation &lt;-
  hemoglobin_distribution_figure_data |&gt;
  dplyr::summarise(
    records =
      dplyr::n(),
    missing_cycle =
      sum(
        is.na(
          survey_cycle_factor
        )
      ),
    missing_sex =
      sum(
        is.na(
          sex
        )
      ),
    missing_hemoglobin =
      sum(
        is.na(
          hemoglobin
        )
      )
  )
Inspect:
print(
  distribution_figure_validation
)
Stop if required values are missing:
if (
  distribution_figure_validation$missing_cycle &gt; 0 ||
  distribution_figure_validation$missing_sex &gt; 0 ||
  distribution_figure_validation$missing_hemoglobin &gt; 0
) {
  stop(
    "The distribution figure data contain missing required values."
  )
}

Why show analytical record counts?
The number of records contributing to a mean can vary by:

cycle;
sex;
eligibility;
missingness;
examination completion.

Displaying record counts helps readers interpret changes in precision and group coverage.

Validate record-count data
Run:
invalid_record_counts &lt;-
  hemoglobin_record_count_figure_data |&gt;
  dplyr::filter(
    is.na(
      analytical_records
    ) |
      analytical_records &lt;= 0
  )
Expected:
nrow(
  invalid_record_counts
)
[1] 0
Stop if invalid:
if (
  nrow(
    invalid_record_counts
  ) &gt; 0
) {
  stop(
    "The record-count figure contains missing or nonpositive counts."
  )
}

Why visualize missingness?
A trend can be influenced by which records are available.
Missingness may change because of:

eligibility;
specimen collection;
examination completion;
laboratory availability;
release structure;
data quality.

The missingness figure helps readers evaluate whether availability differs across cycles or sex groups.

Validate missingness data
Run:
invalid_missingness_figure_values &lt;-
  hemoglobin_missingness_figure_data |&gt;
  dplyr::filter(
    is.na(
      hemoglobin_missing_percent
    ) |
      hemoglobin_missing_percent &lt; 0 |
      hemoglobin_missing_percent &gt; 100
  )
Expected:
nrow(
  invalid_missingness_figure_values
)
[1] 0
Stop if invalid:
if (
  nrow(
    invalid_missingness_figure_values
  ) &gt; 0
) {
  stop(
    "The missingness figure contains invalid percentages."
  )
}

Stop if files are missing
Run:
missing_hemoglobin_figures &lt;-
  hemoglobin_figure_output_check |&gt;
  dplyr::filter(
    !exists
  )
if (
  nrow(
    missing_hemoglobin_figures
  ) &gt; 0
) {
  stop(
    "Expected figure files are missing: ",
    paste(
      missing_hemoglobin_figures$file,
      collapse = ", "
    )
  )
}

Stop if files are empty
Run:
empty_hemoglobin_figures &lt;-
  hemoglobin_figure_output_check |&gt;
  dplyr::filter(
    exists,
    is.na(
      size_bytes
    ) |
      size_bytes &lt;= 0
  )
if (
  nrow(
    empty_hemoglobin_figures
  ) &gt; 0
) {
  stop(
    "Expected figure files are empty: ",
    paste(
      empty_hemoglobin_figures$file,
      collapse = ", "
    )
  )
}

Create figure file hashes
Run:
hemoglobin_figure_hashes &lt;-
  tibble::tibble(
    file =
      hemoglobin_figure_files,
    sha256 =
      purrr::map_chr(
        hemoglobin_figure_files,
        function(file_path) {
          digest::digest(
            file_path,
            algorithm = "sha256",
            file = TRUE
          )
        }
      )
  )
Validate:
if (
  any(
    nchar(
      hemoglobin_figure_hashes$sha256
    ) != 64
  )
) {
  stop(
    "One or more figure hashes are invalid."
  )
}
Save:
readr::write_csv(
  hemoglobin_figure_hashes,
  file.path(
    "outputs",
    "validation",
    "hemoglobin_figure_hashes.csv"
  )
)

Stop when a figure check fails
Run:
failed_hemoglobin_figure_checks &lt;-
  hemoglobin_figure_validation |&gt;
  dplyr::filter(
    !passed
  )
if (
  nrow(
    failed_hemoglobin_figure_checks
  ) &gt; 0
) {
  stop(
    "Hemoglobin figure validation failed: ",
    paste(
      failed_hemoglobin_figure_checks$check,
      collapse = ", "
    )
  )
}
Save:
readr::write_csv(
  hemoglobin_figure_validation,
  file.path(
    "outputs",
    "validation",
    "hemoglobin_figure_validation.csv"
  )
)
Expected message:
message(
  "All professional hemoglobin figures were created, exported, and validated successfully."
)

Using theme_minimal()
A publication figure does not require theme_minimal().
This lesson uses a custom theme with:

white backgrounds;
no gridlines;
visible axis lines;
carefully controlled text and margins.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lesson, you should be able to:

explain the difference between RDS and CSV outputs;
save validated datasets in reusable formats;
preserve R-specific metadata;
create compact analysis-ready extracts;
save variable dictionaries and validation reports;
record package versions and session information;
create an output manifest;
validate that every required output exists.

Why output design matters
A reproducible pipeline should not end with objects that exist only in the current R session.
The results should be saved in formats appropriate for:

future R analysis;
review in spreadsheet software;
sharing with collaborators;
validation and audit;
documentation;
reproducibility;
downstream reporting.

Different output formats serve different purposes.

RDS versus CSV

RDS
An RDS file preserves one R object.
It can preserve:

variable classes;
factors;
ordered factors;
labels;
dates;
logical variables;
list columns;
other R-specific attributes.

Read an RDS file with:
readRDS(
  "path/to/file.rds"
)


CSV
A CSV file is broadly accessible.
It can be opened with:

R;
Python;
Excel;
Google Sheets;
database tools;
text editors.

However, CSV does not preserve:

R factor levels;
ordered-factor status;
variable-label attributes;
object classes beyond plain text and numbers;
other R-specific metadata.

For this reason, the pipeline saves both:
RDS for analytical fidelity
CSV for portability

Where you are in the workflow
You currently have:
cbc_demographics_labelled
This is the validated and labelled merged dataset.
You also have:
cbc_second_exam_2001_2002
This is the supplementary second CBC examination preserved separately.
You have also created:

source manifests;
duplicate-audit reports;
merge-validation reports;
final validation tables;
variable dictionaries.

Save the complete labelled dataset as RDS
Run:
saveRDS(
  cbc_demographics_labelled,
  final_labelled_rds_path
)
This is the preferred reusable R object because it preserves:

variable labels;
factors;
ordered survey cycles;
logical variables;
provenance columns;
validation fields.

Validate the compact dataset row count
The analysis extract should retain every row from the labelled dataset.
Run:
analysis_extract_validation &lt;- tibble::tibble(
  complete_dataset_rows =
    nrow(
      cbc_demographics_labelled
    ),
  analysis_dataset_rows =
    nrow(
      cbc_demographics_analysis
    ),
  rows_preserved =
    complete_dataset_rows ==
      analysis_dataset_rows
)
Inspect:
print(
  analysis_extract_validation
)
Stop if rows differ:
if (
  !analysis_extract_validation$rows_preserved
) {
  stop(
    "The analysis extract does not preserve the complete dataset row count."
  )
}

Create a cycle-level dataset summary
Run:
analysis_cycle_summary &lt;-
  cbc_demographics_analysis |&gt;
  dplyr::group_by(
    survey_cycle
  ) |&gt;
  dplyr::summarise(
    records =
      dplyr::n(),
    unique_participants =
      dplyr::n_distinct(
        SEQN
      ),
    hemoglobin_nonmissing =
      sum(
        !is.na(
          LBXHGB
        )
      ),
    hemoglobin_missing =
      sum(
        is.na(
          LBXHGB
        )
      ),
    matched_demographics =
      sum(
        demographic_match
      ),
    unmatched_demographics =
      sum(
        !demographic_match
      ),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) |&gt;
  dplyr::arrange(
    survey_cycle_factor
  )
If survey_cycle_factor is not retained after summarizing, use:
analysis_cycle_summary &lt;-
  cbc_demographics_analysis |&gt;
  dplyr::group_by(
    survey_cycle,
    survey_cycle_factor
  ) |&gt;
  dplyr::summarise(
    records =
      dplyr::n(),
    unique_participants =
      dplyr::n_distinct(
        SEQN
      ),
    hemoglobin_nonmissing =
      sum(
        !is.na(
          LBXHGB
        )
      ),
    hemoglobin_missing =
      sum(
        is.na(
          LBXHGB
        )
      ),
    matched_demographics =
      sum(
        demographic_match
      ),
    unmatched_demographics =
      sum(
        !demographic_match
      ),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) |&gt;
  dplyr::arrange(
    survey_cycle_factor
  )
Inspect:
print(
  analysis_cycle_summary,
  n = Inf
)
Save:
readr::write_csv(
  analysis_cycle_summary,
  file.path(
    "outputs",
    "tables",
    "analysis_cycle_summary.csv"
  )
)

Record package versions
Define packages used by the pipeline:
pipeline_packages &lt;- c(
  "dplyr",
  "tidyr",
  "purrr",
  "readr",
  "haven",
  "stringr",
  "tibble",
  "ggplot2",
  "digest",
  "httr"
)
Create a version table:
package_versions &lt;- tibble::tibble(
  package = pipeline_packages,
  installed = purrr::map_lgl(
    pipeline_packages,
    requireNamespace,
    quietly = TRUE
  ),
  version = purrr::map_chr(
